   "plaque": "arts_blasket_writers",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "The Blasket Writers",
   "text": "The Great Blasket, the island off the end of the peninsula, never had more than about 175 people, and three of them wrote classics. Tom\u00e1s \u00d3 Criomhthain's An tOile\u00e1nach, The Islandman, came out in 1929; Muiris \u00d3 S\u00failleabh\u00e1in's Fiche Blian ag F\u00e1s, Twenty Years A-Growing, in 1933; and Peig Sayers told her life to her son for Peig in 1936, which every Irish schoolchild then read for fifty years, with mixed feelings. The last islanders were brought ashore on 17 November 1953. 'N\u00ed bheidh \u00e1r leith\u00e9id\u00ed ar\u00eds ann,' wrote Tom\u00e1s: our like will not be there again."
  }
